  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    A blood-working performed by The Communion, a Nightshelf cult sworn to deep-memory communion, at The Bastion Apex during the Frozen Peace. The Communion's elt-sworn misused fire-cores — not for forge-work or transformation but as instruments of opening — at a site the Nightshelf had spent three generations cleansing of older contamination. The ritual invoked Mandatum∴absconditu, the doctrinal creed that survival demands concealment, extending its principle to the ice-memory itself: the event registered only after completion, an act of deliberate archival erasure that I find professionally difficult to forgive.
    
    The dark energy that rippled outward left the Apex's deepest chambers permanently altered. The chronicle source describes twelve footprints frozen into the sub-ice, unthawed offerings, and air carrying a persistent copper scent. Something moved in the water afterward — survivors consistently refuse to name it, describing it only by negation: not orca, not memory, not anything catalogued.
    
    Kaela, a master ice-carver present during or shortly after the event, reportedly descended to the corrupted chamber and addressed whatever remained there. The chronicle records her as apologizing — not for the ritual, which was not hers, but for the inability to undo what had been called forth. Kaela no longer works below the third ice-shelf. When asked why, she checks younger miners' lamp-oil with shaking flippers and says nothing.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771589360211_0", anchor: "an act of deliberate archival erasure that I find professionally difficult to forgive", text: "*Professionally.* I chose that modifier and it is wrong. The ice beneath The Bastion Apex has a gap — not silence but the specific texture of deliberate absence. I have found three such gaps in forty years of reading. The other two predate penguin civilization by strata I cannot date. The Communion learned this technique from somewhere, and the somewhere frightens me more than the ritual itself.", type: :tangent, display: :full
